{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/pulls/1502","id":4501720677,"node_id":"PR_kwDOBtQiWM8AAAABDFLOZQ","html_url":"https://github.com/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/pull/1502","diff_url":"https://github.com/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/pull/1502.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/pull/1502.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/issues/1502","number":1502,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"Add an optional wheel rotational degree of freedom to BOGEY contacts","user":{"login":"Felipegalind0","id":44674864,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0Njc0ODY0","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44674864?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0","html_url":"https://github.com/Felipegalind0","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Felipegalind0/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Summary\n\nAdds an **opt-in wheel rotational degree of freedom** for `BOGEY` contacts. When a contact declares both new elements, its wheel spin rate is resolved together with the existing ground friction Lagrange multipliers in `FGAccelerations::CalculateFrictionForces`:\n\n```xml\n<contact type=\"BOGEY\" name=\"LEFT_MAIN\">\n  <!-- existing elements unchanged -->\n  <wheel_radius unit=\"M\"> 0.22 </wheel_radius>\n  <wheel_inertia unit=\"KG*M2\"> 0.124 </wheel_inertia>\n</contact>\n```\n\nToday `wheel-speed-fps` follows the contact-point velocity kinematically, so wheels never spin up and touchdown spin-up drag is not modelled. With the new DOF:\n\n- the tire tread force acts on the tread slip (axle ground speed − radius × spin), bounded by `static_friction` × normal force;\n- that force reaches the airframe **through the axle** (moment arm = axle, not contact point) while the wheel receives the opposite torque, so the spin-up momentum comes out of the aircraft instead of being double-counted;\n- brakes and rolling resistance become a torque between the wheel and the airframe (a new multiplier with a pure moment Jacobian), bounded by the existing braking friction force × radius. A braked wheel therefore holds the same force as the current anti-skid model, and steady rolling drag is unchanged.\n\nWheels in ground contact at initialization or trim start rolling without slip. In the air they spin down at the same 13 ft/s² tread rate as `wheel-speed-fps`, faster with brakes. New properties: `gear/unit[i]/wheel-spin-rad_sec` and `gear/unit[i]/wheel-tread-slip-fps`.\n\n## Why\n\nThis comes from [OSFS](https://0sfs.github.io/) ([source](https://github.com/0SFS/0SFS.github.io)), an open-source browser flight simulator that runs JSBSim through WebAssembly. We added touchdown feedback (wheel spin-up visuals, tire sound, and gamepad/phone haptics) and wanted the wheels to act on the aircraft, rather than stay a one-way visual model:\n- spin-up drag at touchdown;\n- braking applied through the wheel.\n\nGear friction is resolved inside `CalculateFrictionForces` without a wheel rotation state. An application-side tire model would therefore double-count JSBSim's rolling and brake friction, and JavaScript cannot join the same constraint solve. So the only correct place for the wheel DOF was inside that solve, which is what this PR adds.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `LagrangeMultiplier` gains optional fields: an explicit `MomentJacobian` (for torques that are not `r × F`) and a pointer to a `WheelSpinDOF` with the multiplier's Jacobian entry on the wheel spin.\n- `FGAccelerations::CalculateFrictionForces` assembles `J M⁻¹ Jᵀ` and the right-hand side with the generalized rows (linear, angular, wheel) **only when some multiplier uses them**. Otherwise the original code path runs unchanged, so existing aircraft are unaffected.\n- `FGLGear` parses the elements, owns the spin state, registers the roll row with the axle moment and wheel coupling plus the new wheel-brake row, integrates the spin with the previous solve's acceleration (in step with `FGPropagate`), and documents the elements in the class reference.\n\n## Testing\n\n- New `tests/TestWheelSpin.py`, registered in `tests/CMakeLists.txt`, using a sandboxed copy of `c172p` with the elements added:\n  - opt-in, and invalid values disabling only that contact;\n  - touchdown from an approach: no spin before contact, tread slip gone within 12 steps, no overshoot of rolling speed, and the extra loss of forward momentum matching the wheels' angular momentum;\n  - rollout and braking deceleration within 5% of the unmodified model;\n  - a ground start with no slip;\n  - airborne spin-down with and without brakes.\n- It fails against the current master, and passes with this change.\n- `ctest` on macOS arm64 (Apple clang, Python 3.14, Cython 3.3, CxxTest 4.4), built from master `d0d8bc6e`: every test passes with this change (78 including `TestWheelSpin`). The exception is `TestInputSocket`, which times out in `telnetlib3`'s connection on this machine and passed 1 of 6 repeated runs both with and without the change. At the commit the patch was written against (`1a2e114d`), the full suite passed 76/76 without and 77/77 with it.\n- Unmodified aircraft keep identical behaviour. With and without this change, a native build ran all 61 scripts in `scripts/`, plus a per-step log of position, velocity, attitude and gear forces/moments (1.8 M rows). All data outputs were byte-identical (checked at `1a2e114d`; the diff applies unchanged to `d0d8bc6e`). A WebAssembly build of c172p touchdown/rollout/braking/taxi/cruise traces is also bit-identical.\n\nThe C172 radii and inertias in the test are estimates from Goodyear's aviation data book (6.00-6 main, 5.00-5 nose tires), not measured assemblies. They only need to be plausible for the test.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","created_at":"2026-09-11T01:52:54Z","updated_at":"2026-09-18T14:57:26Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"11f16fabe7238c7e80d2072765c5bea825a8698d","assignees":[{"login":"bcoconni","id":7502904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc1MDI5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7502904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni","html_url":"https://github.com/bcoconni","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bcoconni/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/pulls/1502/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/pulls/1502/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JSBSim-Team/jsbsim/issues/1502/comments","s